## Phile 30. Layers of OPSEC

Choosing the appropriate level of operational security is only one part of the equation. In practice, OPSEC should be considered across multiple layers. A weakness in any single layer can undermine the protection provided by the others.

For most users, it is useful to think about three primary layers:

### Hardware

The hardware layer forms the foundation of your security. If an attacker can compromise your device physically or at the firmware level, software-based protections become much less effective.

Examples include:

Trusted hardware
Secure Boot
Hardware security modules (such as TPMs)
Air-gapped devices for highly sensitive information

### Operating System

The operating system determines how securely your applications run and how well your data is protected.

Depending on your threat model, you may choose different operating systems or configurations, such as:

Privacy-focused Linux distributions
Live operating systems such as Tails
Hardened operating systems
Full-disk encryption

### Applications

Applications are the layer users interact with every day. Even a secure operating system can be undermined by insecure software or poor operational practices.

Examples include:

End-to-end encrypted messaging applications
Privacy-focused web browsers
Password managers
VPNs
Tor Browser
Secure email providers

### Final Takeaway
Every layer should support the others. Using privacy-friendly applications on an insecure operating system—or running a secure operating system on compromised hardware—significantly reduces the effectiveness of your overall OPSEC. The strength of your operational security is determined by its weakest layer.